 tells  people  that there is hope in every place  especially in the most downtrodden  places in the in the world  and we can be the answers to the  question that we seek phoebe what does  punching the air mean  well punching the air can be both an act  of frustration  punching something that you can't see  but it's definitely there  and it can be victorious as you're  punching towards the sky that you've  defeated something great so it was a  perfect title for this book  yeah i hadn't thought of punching there  as being something victorious i also  like  the writing device you use because it's  a novel told in poetry  i think you call it written in verse how  did you two decide that that was the  device  a novel inverse how did you two decide  that that's how you wanted to tell this  particular story this way  well the foundation was already there  when i ran into yousef  evie you you start and then yourself you  pick it up go ahead  when i ran into youssef three years ago  he was selling his self-published book  of poetry that  he had written while incarcerated so the  foundation was already there  um i took one of the most powerful poems  that he had written and built the novel  around that  what were you going to say usa  yeah i was going to say that the great  thing about poetry especially written  this way is that it pulls the  reader through and allows them it keeps  their mind attentive  the words on the page everything is  beautiful about the book punching the  air  this piece that we created is really  really amazing  and you know to do it in poetic format  you know  life is poetry you know that's one of  the best things that we could do  evie i think it's i'm curious about how  the two of you got together because i  know you met in college  and he he at the time was out of prison  but he had not yet been exotic  not yet been exonerated right  yeah absolutely we met at new york  city's hunter college  and uh we always knew that there was  something not right about the case we  knew  that they were innocent and at the time  i was the editor of my college's  newspaper  and i remember the story from when i was  in sixth grade  and when i saw him i just had to get an  interview i never got that interview but  i ran into him again three years ago  and here we are with his novel were you  afraid you have to tell your story  you know when i came home from prison i  was hiding in plain sight  you know here i am over six feet tall  and i had this indelible scar that the  system had labeled me a rapist and i was  very very scared  of who to tell you know my truth to and  you know because it wasn't popular you  know donald trump had taken out that  full page ad  i remember years ago that was 31 years  ago and  i mean i was walking around with that  target on my back at all times  and you talk about the importance and so  does the mall  it's okay to be angry but not be bitter  how were you able to get to that place  and do that well i tell you we you know  i listened to the words of  you know mandiba um nelson mandela and  what he said was he had to leave anger  and bitterness in the prison  because if he took it outside with him  it would destroy him right and then i  heard the words of dr maya angelou who  said  you should be angry but you must not be  bitter and then she taught us how to  you know take that anger and turn it  into something by saying you know  it it can metastasize into something but  if you  use that anger and dance it and write it  and  be you know do everything about it talk  about it talk it never stop talking it  you get the opportunity to take the  the horns of the bull and really direct  it in a manner that really can change  lives i'm wondering if this was a  personal story for you in some way you  said you  i just heard you say you knew this story  as a little girl then you meet him in  college and then you end up  working with them on working with him on  this book  was it personal for you  eb eb  eb can you hear me eb can you hear me  you lost her i think we lost eb this is  what happens in live tv  you can't hear me well i'll tell you  okay go ahead go ahead you know part of  the  yeah part of the conversation that eb  and i had which was really important i  think  because here i am thinking that i'm  going through this in a vacuum that this  is only happening to me  and meeting evie and having the  conversation with her about all of these  injustices that were going on you know  you had amadou diallo  sean bell you had i mean even now we  have so many names that are coming up to  our doorstep  today that it was amazing that she was  able  to realize wow you know he looks like a  boy in my classroom  yeah and that's the connecting that she  was making that all of like all of us  all of the five of us  look like people and were people we  represented the microcosm of the  macrocosm of people just like us  all right yusof salaam thank you so much  for joining us eb saboy  teller we said thank you too the name of  the book is called punching the air  it goes on sale tomorrow wherever you  like to buy your books

